# FACTORY (the author, 2026-10-05): `@: h f(a)` receives a factory's result; this row wrote `h: f a` until then.
# OPEN, a probe of what the translator does today and not a norm. make3
# declares its result as fn: (int: p; int: q) int and returns f3, whose own
# formals are x and y. The call h3(q: 2; p: 1) is bound by the names of the
# declared header and reaches f3 by coordinate: 200 + 10 + 2. Whether a named
# use by p and q is accepted by the interface of the callable f3 actually is,
# is not settled: the actual descriptor has the names x and y, and no rule
# that adapts one set of names to the other has been shown. Entry 7.
fn: make3 (int: n) fn: (int: p; int: q) int
    fn: f3 (int: x; int: y) int
        return: n + x * 10 + y
    return: f3

@: h3 make3(200)
int: r h3(q: 2; p: 1)
int: ok 7
if: r != 212
    ok: 81
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
